== The Architect: Modlist

Wave 1 is where the factory stops being *good* and starts being *scalable*. The Castaway proved the rocket; The Architect has to prove the factory behind it — belt highways, a signalling rail network, circuit logic, robot logistics, modules and quality at volume. The mods in this chapter exist to deepen exactly those scaling systems, and none of them hands out power. Every card below passes the power-spike test: nothing raises an output cap, a throughput ceiling, an efficiency number or a cost for free. Where a mod genuinely increases what a belt or a building can carry, the increase is bought with research, materials, and a real logistics step the engineer has to build and pay for. If a mod ever started giving vanilla numbers away, it would fail this wave's one rule.

Eleven mods ship in Wave 1, split across the next three sections by what they change. *Content* carries the three logistics and production mods — Miniloader (Redux), Deadlock's Stacking Beltboxes & Compact Loaders, and Warehousing Mod — the new belts, loaders and containers the scaling factory is built from. *Mechanics* carries the four system mods — Project Cybersyn, Circuit HUD V2, Module Inserter and Auto Deconstruct — the train, circuit, module and expansion-lifecycle systems that drive the factory. *Graphics* carries the four visual mods — Nixie Tubes, Belt Visualizer, Prismatic Belts and Circuit Visualizer — the signal and overlay clarity that keeps a megabase readable. All eleven are verified on the official Factorio Mod Portal against Factorio 2.0.77; each card records the mod's last-update date, its 2.0-line build, and its dependencies, and every verdict is logged in STATUS.md.
